WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.110
<v Maya>Welcome to part three.

2
00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:06.940
<v Maya>So far we covered the objectives, screening, the order of tests and blood pressure.

3
00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.480
<v Maya>Today we start the longest component, body composition.

4
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.970
<v Dev>Longest, why?

5
00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.180
<v Maya>Because body composition has eight tests, grouped into three categories.

6
00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:23.940
<v Maya>Today we do the first category, anthropometric data, which has three tests, waist circumference, waist to hip ratio and BMI.

7
00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.780
<v Maya>Here are the three categories.

8
00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:32.730
<v Maya>Category two point one is anthropometric data, with waist circumference, waist to hip ratio and BMI.

9
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:34.220
<v Maya>No machine needed.

10
00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:38.670
<v Maya>Category two point two is skinfold measurement, with the skinfold caliper.

11
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:47.220
<v Maya>Category two point three is the use of machines, with BIA, hydrostatic or underwater weighing, the Bod Pod, and DEXA.

12
00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:50.500
<v Dev>That's three plus one plus four, eight tests.

13
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:51.400
<v Maya>Yes.

14
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:55.810
<v Maya>The number two stands for the second type of fitness test, body composition.

15
00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:03.090
<v Maya>Then two point one is the first category, two point one point one to two point one point three are its tests, and so on.

16
00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.670
<v Maya>It helps you remember which test belongs where.

17
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.060
<v Maya>And the goal for now is to be aware of all eight, not to conduct all eight.

18
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.670
<v Dev>And for each test we follow a pattern?

19
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.630
<v Maya>Objective, procedure, norms or results, then advantages and disadvantages.

20
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.210
<v Maya>Anthropo means the human body, metric means measurement.

21
00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:26.940
<v Maya>Anthropometry is the scientific study of the measurements and proportions of the human body.

22
00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:28.460
<v Maya>Three key measurements.

23
00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:30.400
<v Maya>Height, with a stadiometer.

24
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.170
<v Maya>Weight, with a weighing scale.

25
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.090
<v Maya>And circumference, the length of the boundary of something, with a tape, like a tailor's tape.

26
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.360
<v Dev>How do I measure height properly?

27
00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.820
<v Maya>Bare feet, standing tall and straight, shoulders relaxed, hands at the sides.

28
00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.880
<v Maya>Heels, buttocks and shoulder blades touch the board.

29
00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.020
<v Maya>Lower the headboard until it touches the crown of the head.

30
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.950
<v Maya>Record to the nearest quarter inch or centimetre.

31
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.980
<v Maya>For weight, shoes off, stand in the centre of the scale, still, weight even on both feet.

32
00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.890
<v Dev>What does each test tell you?

33
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:06.380
<v Maya>Waist circumference tells you whether there's a health risk because of visceral fat, also called central obesity.

34
00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:09.760
<v Maya>Waist to hip ratio tells you the overall health risk.

35
00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:14.930
<v Maya>BMI places you in a category, underweight, normal, overweight or obese.

36
00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.400
<v Dev>Visceral fat is the deep fat?

37
00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:22.060
<v Maya>Yes, the deep fat towards the centre, which is why it's called central obesity.

38
00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.100
<v Maya>Subcutaneous fat lies towards the skin.

39
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:29.680
<v Maya>Important point: waist circumference only says whether risk is high or low.

40
00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.560
<v Maya>It does not give a visceral fat percentage.

41
00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.370
<v Dev>Where does the tape go for waist circumference?

42
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:41.530
<v Maya>Across the iliac crest, which is the highest point of the hip bone, which you can feel if you stand and touch it.

43
00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.270
<v Maya>The tape also passes through the belly button.

44
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.800
<v Maya>That is the widest part of the waist.

45
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.890
<v Dev>And with a client, do I say iliac crest?

46
00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:50.730
<v Maya>No.

47
00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.060
<v Maya>They will say, where?

48
00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:57.040
<v Maya>Say the widest part of your waist, which automatically falls across the iliac crest.

49
00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:01.210
<v Maya>Also, because it involves touch, ask permission, whatever the gender.

50
00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:07.570
<v Maya>If the client isn't comfortable, show the position on your own body and let them place the tape, and you note the reading.

51
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.040
<v Dev>And the norms?

52
00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:21.670
<v Maya>For Indians, applicable to Asians overall, low health risk is below thirty five and a half inches, which is ninety centimetres, for males, and below thirty one and a half inches, which is eighty centimetres, for females.

53
00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.520
<v Maya>The tape has inches on one side and centimetres on the other.

54
00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:28.120
<v Dev>Those aren't invented by the course?

55
00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:32.060
<v Maya>No, they were suggested by research and international bodies.

56
00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:39.360
<v Maya>A note for your course: the E-book uses higher cut offs, above one hundred and two centimetres for men and eighty eight for women.

57
00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:42.950
<v Maya>Use ninety and eighty for Asians as the course teaches.

58
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.770
<v Maya>Now waist to hip ratio.

59
00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.920
<v Maya>The formula is waist circumference divided by hip circumference.

60
00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:54.190
<v Maya>Both must be in the same unit, inches with inches or centimetres with centimetres.

61
00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.170
<v Dev>Same waist as before?

62
00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.100
<v Maya>Careful.

63
00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.010
<v Maya>The waist site here is different.

64
00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:05.190
<v Maya>For this test, use the narrowest part of the waist, the midpoint between the last rib and the iliac crest.

65
00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.700
<v Maya>The hip is the widest part of the hips.

66
00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:12.330
<v Maya>Plain wording for clients: narrow part of the waist, widest part of the hips.

67
00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:13.510
<v Dev>Norms?

68
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:19.570
<v Maya>Low risk is below zero point nine five for males and below zero point eight six for females.

69
00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:20.750
<v Maya>Worked case.

70
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.290
<v Maya>A male client has a waist of thirty six inches and hips of forty inches.

71
00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:29.220
<v Maya>Thirty six divided by forty equals zero point nine zero.

72
00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:36.080
<v Maya>The male limit is zero point nine five, so zero point nine zero is below it, low overall health risk.

73
00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.810
<v Dev>What if it's a woman with the same numbers?

74
00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:45.780
<v Maya>The female limit is zero point eight six, and zero point nine zero is bigger than that, so high overall risk.

75
00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.260
<v Maya>Same number, different result by sex.

76
00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.120
<v Maya>That is why the limits differ.

77
00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.290
<v Maya>Quick recap.

78
00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:59.350
<v Maya>Waist circumference is visceral fat risk, tape at the widest part of the waist, below ninety centimetres for men and eighty for women.

79
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:09.930
<v Maya>Waist to hip ratio is overall risk, waist divided by hip, narrowest waist, widest hips, below zero point nine five for men, zero point eight six for women.

80
00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:11.290
<v Dev>Pause and think.

81
00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:15.640
<v Dev>A woman has a waist of thirty inches and hips of thirty six inches.

82
00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.080
<v Dev>Her ratio?

83
00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:18.290
<v Maya>You tell me.

84
00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.180
<v Dev>Thirty divided by thirty six is zero point eight three.

85
00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:25.630
<v Dev>The limit is zero point eight six, so low risk.

86
00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.520
<v Maya>Exactly.

87
00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:28.740
<v Maya>BMI uses weight and height.

88
00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:34.390
<v Maya>Body mass index equals body weight in kilograms divided by height in metres, squared.

89
00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.870
<v Maya>The unit is kilograms per square metre.

90
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:40.610
<v Maya>Squaring means multiplying the number by itself once.

91
00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.720
<v Dev>Height usually comes in feet and inches though.

92
00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:44.920
<v Maya>So convert.

93
00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:46.890
<v Maya>Say five feet eight inches.

94
00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:52.370
<v Maya>One foot is twelve inches, so five feet is sixty inches, plus eight is sixty eight inches.

95
00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:56.800
<v Maya>Then multiply by zero point zero two five four to get metres.

96
00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:03.040
<v Maya>Sixty eight times zero point zero two five four is one point seven two seven two metres.

97
00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.870
<v Dev>And the lecture example?

98
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.580
<v Maya>The lecture has a client of ninety kilograms and five feet eight.

99
00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:11.590
<v Maya>The board rounded the height to one point seven metres.

100
00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.920
<v Maya>One point seven times one point seven is two point eight nine, written as two point nine.

101
00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:21.170
<v Maya>Ninety divided by two point nine is thirty one point zero three.

102
00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:26.940
<v Maya>With the exact height, one point seven two seven two, the answer is about thirty point one seven.

103
00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:31.210
<v Maya>The slides use one point seven two metres and get thirty point five.

104
00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.430
<v Maya>All obese, so the method matters, not the rounding.

105
00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.040
<v Maya>Check your course material for which version your exam uses.

106
00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:39.800
<v Dev>What are the categories?

107
00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:43.040
<v Maya>The course uses the proposed Asian criteria.

108
00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:45.550
<v Maya>Underweight is below eighteen point five.

109
00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.920
<v Maya>Normal is eighteen point five to twenty two point nine.

110
00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:51.950
<v Maya>Overweight is twenty three to twenty four point nine.

111
00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:54.110
<v Maya>Obese is twenty five and above.

112
00:06:54.160 --> 00:07:01.820
<v Maya>The E-book uses overweight twenty five to twenty nine point nine and obese thirty and above, so remember which one the course teaches.

113
00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.200
<v Dev>Another quick one.

114
00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:07.120
<v Dev>Sixty three kilograms, one point seven five metres.

115
00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:12.770
<v Maya>One point seven five times one point seven five is three point zero six two five.

116
00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:17.090
<v Maya>Sixty three divided by that is twenty point six, which is normal.

117
00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:18.840
<v Dev>Now the shortcomings.

118
00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:20.100
<v Maya>Two big ones.

119
00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:24.410
<v Maya>One, BMI does not differentiate body fat from lean body mass.

120
00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:30.040
<v Maya>Picture a client's details of ninety kilograms and five feet eight arriving before the first meeting.

121
00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:34.060
<v Maya>You calculate, conclude obese, then he walks in and he's very muscular.

122
00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.040
<v Maya>Awkward.

123
00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:42.630
<v Maya>A bodybuilder and an obese person of the same height and weight get the same BMI, because weight is lean mass plus fat.

124
00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:43.880
<v Dev>And two?

125
00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.070
<v Maya>Skinny fat.

126
00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:50.520
<v Maya>A BMI of twenty one is normal, but the arms and legs are lean while there's fat in the belly.

127
00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:54.820
<v Maya>BMI only looks at weight and height, so it calls that person normal.

128
00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:58.260
<v Dev>So none of these three give a body fat percentage.

129
00:07:58.310 --> 00:07:59.160
<v Maya>Right.

130
00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:07.110
<v Maya>Ideal body fat is below fifteen percent for males and below twenty percent for females, so you need a percentage to judge composition.

131
00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:09.130
<v Maya>These tests are not thrown away.

132
00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:18.110
<v Maya>Use them as short term or mid term goals, for example, you are two inches above the normal waist value, so your short term goal is to get below it.

133
00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.540
<v Dev>Let me check I understand the trap.

134
00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:25.860
<v Dev>If a client says they are normal on BMI, that doesn't prove they are healthy.

135
00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:27.000
<v Maya>Exactly.

136
00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:31.710
<v Maya>And if they are obese on BMI, it doesn't prove they carry too much fat.

137
00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:36.150
<v Maya>BMI is a quick screening number, not a body composition test.

138
00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:39.080
<v Dev>So where do these three fit in a real plan?

139
00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:42.010
<v Maya>As simple, no machine, no cost starting points.

140
00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:49.500
<v Maya>Waist circumference gives a visceral fat risk, waist to hip ratio gives the overall risk, BMI gives a category.

141
00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:52.330
<v Maya>Then the next tests go looking for the actual percentage.

142
00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:53.610
<v Maya>Let's wrap up.

143
00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.010
<v Maya>Body composition has eight tests in three categories.

144
00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.170
<v Maya>Today, anthropometry.

145
00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:02.840
<v Dev>Waist circumference, waist to hip ratio, and BMI.

146
00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:04.760
<v Dev>None gives a percentage.

147
00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:06.430
<v Maya>Five things to remember.

148
00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.100
<v Maya>One, eight tests, three categories: anthropometry, skinfold, machines.

149
00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:17.300
<v Maya>Two, waist circumference at the widest part, below ninety centimetres men and eighty centimetres women.

150
00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:25.690
<v Maya>Three, waist to hip ratio is waist divided by hip, narrowest waist, below zero point nine five men, zero point eight six women.

151
00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:31.950
<v Maya>Four, BMI is kilograms divided by metres squared, Asian obese at twenty five and above.

152
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.450
<v Maya>Five, BMI cannot separate fat from muscle and misses skinny fat.

153
00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:39.700
<v Dev>Next, calipers, BIA and Bod Pod.

154
00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:40.990
<v Maya>See you in part four.

